# modules.cpp external CMake builder specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the implemented external CMake builder: how a selected
SDK delegates application linking to a library-owned bridge, the files and
variables exchanged with that bridge, cache identity, ABI verification, and
artifact publication. The manifest declarations that activate it require mm:
1.4.

status: official gives this document the same authority as the other implemented
specifications in docs.

docs/modules-libraries.mdy remains authoritative for kind: library, library
references, checkout validation, and wrapper availability. docs/modules-platforms.mdy
remains authoritative for SDK selection and link ownership in the configuration
record. docs/modules-build.mdy remains authoritative for compilation, application
object closure, lane selection, and build exit status. This document owns the
generic contract between mm.build and an external CMake bridge.

docs/modules-platform-pico.mdy owns the Pico SDK instance of the contract,
including board mapping, picotool, its C adapter, and UF2 validation.

Examples use Opening fence and Closing fence instead of literal front matter
delimiters, following docs/mdy.mdy.

# What the external builder is

CMake is a link/build mechanism, not a platform and not an application property.
An SDK selects a library, the library declares external-build: cmake, and
configure records cross-link: external for the selected target lane. At link
time every available application in that lane is handed to the bridge. An
application neither declares CMake nor becomes a CMake target in its manifest.

Opening fence
selected SDK
  library: cmake-demo
    -> library cmake-demo
       external-build: cmake
         -> cross-link: external
            -> external CMake link for every application in the lane
Closing fence

This lane-wide rule is what lets a portable core application use the runtime
supplied by a platform without declaring that runtime as its own dependency. A
module may separately declare library: and an application may use that wrapper;
those declarations describe source dependencies, not link ownership.

# Activation and validation

A CMake-backed library has this shape:

Opening fence
library/mm.mdy                  kind: library, external-build: cmake
library/LICENSE                 repository-owned licence
library/upstream/               foreign source checkout
library/cmake/CMakeLists.txt    project-owned bridge
library/cmake/mm-requires.txt   optional package requirements
Closing fence

external-build is a single optional mm: 1.4 key valid only on kind: library.
cmake is its only supported value. The cmake directory is found by convention
beside the library manifest; no second manifest key names it. Manifest loading
requires that directory and CMakeLists.txt to exist inside the project and to
resolve outside the foreign source checkout.

An SDK activates the bridge by naming the library:

Opening fence
mm: 1.4
kind: sdk
name: m68k-linux-external
target: m68k-linux-gnu
compiler-family: gcc
runtime: glibc
library: cmake-demo
Closing fence

Selecting that SDK requires the checkout to be present. Configure writes
cross-link: external and omits project-link fields that do not apply. Build
loads the record, resolves the selected SDK and library again, and rejects a
record whose saved ownership disagrees with the live external-build declaration.
Changing either manifest therefore requires reconfiguration rather than silently
changing the link path.

# Application link dispatch

mm.build compiles project modules and application translation units normally.
For an application it computes the same ordered transitive object closure used
by project linking and appends any selected board objects. If the configured
platform owns the link, mm.build invokes its linker directly. If ownership is
external, it calls the CMake builder with that object list.

The external builder resolves the selected SDK's library and verifies that its
external-build value is cmake. It requires configured C and C++ drivers and
resolves both through PATH to canonical executable paths. The app name, not a
manifest-derived CMake identifier, names the output and its isolated build
directory:

Opening fence
out-target-TARGET/external/LIBRARY/BOARD-OR-none/APP/
Closing fence

Each application has its own CMake cache. Two applications in one external lane
therefore configure and build independently in the current implementation.

# Generated input files

Before invoking CMake, mm.build writes mm-inputs.cmake and mm-toolchain.cmake in
the isolated external directory.

mm-inputs.cmake defines exactly these generic values:

- MM_OBJECTS is the ordered list of absolute project and board object paths.
- MM_OUTPUT_NAME is the application name.
- MM_LIBRARY_SOURCE is the absolute foreign source checkout path.
- MM_BOARD is the selected board name, or an empty string for a boardless lane.
- MM_TOOLCHAIN_FILE is the absolute generated toolchain-file path.
- MM_C_COMPILER is the canonical configured C driver.
- MM_CXX_COMPILER is the canonical configured C++ driver.

The file conveys no adapter name, foreign target name, Pico variable, or
library-specific option. The bridge owns all such choices.

Every generated value uses a CMake bracket argument with a delimiter chosen not
to collide with the value. Newlines, carriage returns, and semicolons are
rejected because a bracket argument cannot prevent a semicolon from splitting a
CMake list. Failure to find a delimiter within the implementation's bound is
also an error. These rules apply to object paths, names, source and toolchain
paths, board names, and compiler paths.

mm-toolchain.cmake defines:

- CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME as Generic for a bare-metal lane or Linux for a hosted lane.
- CMAKE_TRY_COMPILE_TARGET_TYPE as STATIC_LIBRARY.
- CMAKE_C_COMPILER and CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER from the configured lane.
- CMAKE_SYSROOT when the platform supplies one.

It deliberately contains no processor options. A bridge may select this file as
its CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE, as cmake-demo does, or use a foreign build's toolchain
selection, as Pico SDK does. Omitting project processor options prevents the
foreign ABI check from passing merely because mm.build injected its own answer.

# Bridge obligations

The bridge is a checked-in CMake project owned by the library integration. It
must:

1. Read mm-inputs.cmake from CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR.
2. Configure its foreign build with the supplied drivers or a compatible
   toolchain derived from them.
3. Treat MM_OBJECTS as generated external objects and preserve their order.
4. Define a build target named mm_external.
5. Link mm_external with the C++ linker, normally with LINKER_LANGUAGE CXX.
6. Enable compile-command export and compile at least one representative C
   source with the foreign build's effective target options.
7. Write mm-abi-probe.txt containing that source's absolute path.
8. Write mm-result.txt containing the primary and optional supplemental outputs.

MM_OBJECTS are passed directly rather than packed into a static archive. This
preserves namespace-scope constructors and self-registering objects which an
archive linker could omit when no ordinary symbol pulls their object from the
archive.

The bridge may generate private sources and artifacts. Their names and meanings
stay inside the bridge; mm.build learns only the ABI probe path and reported
result paths.

# CMake package requirements

A bridge that requires preinstalled CMake config packages declares their
variables in cmake/mm-requires.txt, one per line:

Opening fence
picotool_DIR
Closing fence

Each name must match a CMake-style identifier ending in _DIR. Blank, malformed,
and duplicate lines are rejected. Before CMake runs, each corresponding
environment variable must name an existing directory containing either
PackageConfig.cmake or Package-config.cmake, where Package is the variable name
without _DIR.

mm.build canonicalises each directory, passes it as
-DVariable:PATH=directory, and includes the variable, path, and recursive package
contents in cache identity. No package directory is searched implicitly. CMake
is also invoked with FETCHCONTENT_FULLY_DISCONNECTED=ON, so a bridge cannot turn
a missing local prerequisite into an unrequested download.

The requirement belongs to the bridge rather than to a library name. Renaming a
library does not disable it, and a second CMake library can declare its own
requirements without adding a special case to mm.build.

# Cache identity and configuration

mm.build resolves CMake through PATH and queries the CMake, C, and C++ driver
versions. It fingerprints:

- the identity format and the fixed Unix Makefiles generator;
- the bridge path and recursive bridge contents;
- mm-inputs.cmake and mm-toolchain.cmake;
- every required package variable, canonical path, and recursive contents;
- CMake and compiler canonical paths and reported versions;
- C, C++, and linker argument strings;
- target, system, sysroot, board, and output name; and
- the project's normalized ABI projection.

The fingerprint is stored in mm-cache-identity.txt. If CMakeCache.txt exists and
the identity is absent or different, mm.build first verifies that the directory
is inside the project, removes that application's external directory, recreates
it, and rewrites both generated files. This prevents CMake from retaining a
compiler or lane value after the input changed.

Foreign checkout contents are not recursively fingerprinted. Their path is in
mm-inputs.cmake, while ordinary foreign source changes are left to CMake's own
dependency tracking. Bridge and package contents are fingerprinted because they
control configuration and may otherwise leave stale cache decisions.

Whether the cache was retained or recreated, mm.build configures on every link:

Opening fence
cmake -G "Unix Makefiles"
      -DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON
      -DFETCHCONTENT_FULLY_DISCONNECTED=ON
      [required -DPackage_DIR:PATH=... values]
      -S BRIDGE -B EXTERNAL_DIRECTORY
Closing fence

Verbose mode shows the commands and reports cache invalidation. Non-verbose mode
suppresses CMake configure and build output while preserving the final
modules.cpp diagnostic.

# ABI verification

The external builder refuses to link project and foreign objects until their
calling conventions agree. After configure, it reads mm-abi-probe.txt. The file
must name one source which resolves to exactly one command-form entry in
compile_commands.json by canonical path. The JSON arguments form is unsupported,
and @ response-file arguments are rejected.

The compile command's driver must canonically equal the configured C driver.
mm.build retains only -m options from that command, then asks the same driver for
its effective target state under LC_ALL=C:

Opening fence
C-DRIVER OPTIONS -Q --help=target -c
Closing fence

It performs the same query for the project's processor options and compares a
closed projection for the configured triple:

Opening fence
arm-none-eabi     -march= -mthumb -mfloat-abi= -mfpu= -mcmse
m68k-linux-gnu    -march= -mcpu= -m68881 -mhard-float -msoft-float
riscv32-pico-elf  -march= -mabi=
Closing fence

Every projected field must appear exactly once. Empty values of fields ending
in = are valid driver state; boolean fields must be [enabled] or [disabled]. A
missing, repeated, malformed, or unequal projection stops before
the external link and prints both option sets and projections.

# Build and result publication

After ABI agreement, mm.build runs:

Opening fence
cmake --build EXTERNAL_DIRECTORY --target mm_external
Closing fence

The bridge reports outputs in mm-result.txt, one absolute path per line. The
first line is the primary executable. Later lines are supplemental artifacts.
Every reported path must identify an existing regular file whose canonical path
is inside the isolated external directory. Sources must be unique; blank
interior lines, symlink escapes, and destination collisions are errors.

The primary result is copied atomically to the ordinary extensionless
application path. A supplemental file name must start with MM_OUTPUT_NAME and
continue with a dot-prefixed suffix. That suffix is preserved exactly:

Opening fence
app.elf.map -> published-name.elf.map
app.bin     -> published-name.bin
app.uf2     -> published-name.uf2
Closing fence

Run and debug consume the normal primary path and do not need to know that CMake
linked it. Supplemental formats remain platform-defined.

# Implemented bridges

libraries/cmake-demo is the small generic fixture. Its bridge selects the
generated toolchain file, compiles one foreign C source, links the project
objects into mm_external, and reports one executable. The
m68k-linux-external SDK selects it. Its cmake-demo-smoke application uses two
project translation units so a successful QEMU exit proves that a namespace-scope
constructor survived the object hand-off.

platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk is the production integration. Its bridge maps the
selected Pico board, lets Pico SDK select its own platform toolchain, compiles a
project-owned C adapter, and reports ELF, BIN, HEX, MAP, and validated UF2
artifacts. It declares picotool_DIR through mm-requires.txt. None of those Pico
details appears in the generic builder.

# Boundaries

- Only CMake and the Unix Makefiles generator are supported.
- Only target lanes may select external link ownership; the host platform is
  project-linked.
- Each application has a separate CMake configure/build directory. External SDK
  compilation is not shared between executables.
- Link ownership is lane-wide. There is no application-level external-build key.
- Applications cannot name libraries directly. They depend on project wrapper
  modules through use; a wrapper may name a library.
- requires-sdk is not implemented, so a boardless SDK-specific application
  cannot express that availability constraint directly in its manifest.
- The compile-database reader intentionally supports only the narrow command
  form generated by the pinned bridges; it is not a general compilation database
  library.

# Conformance

tests/mm/build/external.cpp exercises generated input escaping, package
requirements, projection schemas and parsing, live driver queries, cache identity,
and result validation. tests/mm/build/configuration.cpp and
tests/mm/configure/write_configuration.cpp exercise external link records and
staleness rules.

The m68k-linux-external fixture proves configuration, external CMake linking,
artifact publication, and execution under qemu-user without Pico SDK. The ARM
and RISC-V Pico SDK integration scripts prove the production bridges with real
cross compilers and picotool. Requirements not reached by one of those tests
remain review-enforced.
